   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to authorize certain health care professionals licensed to practice in\nother jurisdictions to practice in this state in connection with an event\nsanctioned by the World Triathlon Corporation; and providing for the repeal of\nsuch provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8409",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8409-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Part XXX Subpart B Item A §§1 & 2, Chap 58 of 2020",
    "lawSection": "Public Health",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8409\n\nSPONSOR: LITTLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to authorize certain health care professionals licensed to prac-\ntice in other jurisdictions to practice in this state in connection with\nan event sanctioned by the World Triathlon Corporation; and providing\nfor the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation allows health care professionals to perform services at\nthe Ironman triathlon and Ironman 70.3 to be held in 2020 in Lake Plac-\nid, New York.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 - allows any person who is licensed to practice as a physi-\ncian, physician's assistant, massage therapist, physical therapist,\nchiropractor, dentist, optometrist, nurse, nurse practitioner, emergency\nmedical technician or podiatrist in another state or territory, who is\nin good standing in such state or territory, and who has been appointed\nby World Triathlon Corporation to provide professional services to\ncompetitors at these Ironman events.\n\nSection 2 - provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe triathlon in Lake Placid is a one day 2.4-mile swim and 112-mile\nbike ride which concludes with a 26.2-mile marathon. As this event is\nphysically taxing on the athlete, the health and safety of the partic-\nipants in the days before the event and especially during and after the\nevent are crucial. This legislation would allow licensed health care\nprofessionals from other states who volunteer and are sanctioned by\nWorld Triathlon Corporation to perform services for the athletes.\n\nEspecially important at Ironman events is the massage tent that is\navailable to the athletes following the race where muscle cramps and\nminor aches and pains can be attended to. Without this immediate atten-\ntion, many of these competitors would require further intensive medical\nintervention. The average time an athlete spends in this area is approx-\nimately seven minutes, which is not a formal massage treatment by any\nmeans. To service the 1,500 athletes, licensed volunteers from other\nstates are necessary. Ironman 70.3 which is the same format as the Iron-\nman but half the distance.\n\nDue to the Covid-19 health pandemic this year these events will be held\nsometime in 2020 in Lake Placid, New York. Dates are to be determined.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nLegislation of this type has been passed by the Legislature in the past\nfor the Olympic Games in Lake Placid in 1980 and the Goodwill Games in\nNew York City in 1997 as well as for the 2001, 2002, 2003, 2004, 2005,\n2006, 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017,\n2018 and 2019 Ironman USA triathlons in Lake Placid.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed December 31, 2020.\n",
